 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\nRequest object no longer sanitizes sensitive information in __repr__\n\nDescription\n\nWhen creating a Request object with sensitive information like passwords, tokens, or authorization headers, this information is now exposed in the string representation of the object.\n\nFor example, when creating a Request with a password in the body:\n\n```python\nfrom oauthlib.common import Request\n\npayload = 'username=foo&password=mysecretpassword'\nr = Request('https://example.com', body=payload)\nprint(repr(r))\n```\n\nThis now outputs:\n```\n<Request None https://example.com headers={} body=username=foo&password=mysecretpassword>\n```\n\nPreviously, sensitive information would be sanitized and the output would look like:\n```\n<oauthlib.Request url=\"https://example.com\", http_method=\"None\", headers=\"{}\", body=\"username=foo&password=<SANITIZED>\">\n```\n\nThe same issue occurs with:\n- Authorization headers (credentials are exposed)\n- refresh_token values in the body\n- Other sensitive parameters\n\nSteps to reproduce:\n\n1. Create a Request object with sensitive information:\n```python\nfrom oauthlib.common import Request\n\n# With password in body\nr1 = Request('https://example.com', body='username=foo&password=secret')\nprint(repr(r1))\n\n# With Authorization header\nr2 = Request('https://example.com', headers={'Authorization': 'Basic Zm9vOmJhcg=='})\nprint(repr(r2))\n\n# With token in body\nr3 = Request('https://example.com', body='client_id=foo&refresh_token=secret_token')\nprint(repr(r3))\n```\n\nThis is a security issue as sensitive information could be accidentally logged or exposed in error messages.\n</issue>\n",
